{"cuisine/cuisine-of-california":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/cuisine-of-california","n":"Cuisine of California","c":"#94bd5c","crumb":[["The Americas","cuisines#the-americas"],["Western American cuisine","cuisine/cuisine-of-the-western-united-states"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in United States of America","intro":[["The cuisine of California reflects the diverse culture of California and is influenced largely by Indigenous, European American, Hispanic American  (Mexican, Latin American, Spanish), East Asian and Oceanian influences (Japanese, Chinese, Korean, Filipino, Vietnamese), and Middle Eastern and Mediterranean influences, as well as the food trends and traditions of larger American cuisine."],["The main trends were influenced by a combination of Mediterranean climate, geography and geology of the region's proximity to the ocean, its movie roots in Hollywood, its technology roots in Silicon Valley and the Napa wine country, as well as its major produce production. California used to be part of Mexico, which influenced the state's food."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"cuisine-of-california"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cuisine_of_California"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Western American cuisine","cuisine/cuisine-of-the-western-united-states"]]},{"h":"Dishes","count":4,"links":[["Celery Victor","dish/celery-victor"],["Green goddess dressing","dish/green-goddess-dressing"],["Hangtown fry","dish/hangtown-fry"],["Oysters Kirkpatrick","dish/oysters-kirkpatrick"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Bacon","ingredient/bacon"],["Oyster","ingredient/oyster"],["Bell peppers","ingredient/bell-peppers"],["Broth","ingredient/broth"],["Celery","ingredient/celery"],["Romaine lettuce","ingredient/romaine-lettuce"],["Anchovy","ingredient/anchovy"],["Black pepper","ingredient/black-pepper"],["Chervil","ingredient/chervil"],["Chives","ingredient/chives"],["Lemon","ingredient/lemon"],["Mayonnaise","ingredient/mayonnaise"],["Sour cream","ingredient/sour-cream"],["Tarragon","ingredient/tarragon"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Cheese","ingredient/cheese"],["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Scallion","ingredient/scallion"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Western American cuisine","cuisine/cuisine-of-the-western-united-states"]]},{"h":"Within Cuisine of California cooking","count":2,"links":[["California cuisine","cuisine/california-cuisine"],["Korean-Mexican fusion","cuisine/korean-mexican-fusion"]]}],"cont":"Cuisine of the Americas","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/f/fd/Flickr_dongkwan_540812245--Shrimp_Tostada.jpg/500px-Flickr_dongkwan_540812245--Shrimp_Tostada.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Ernesto Andrade from San Francisco, California, USA","lic":"CC BY 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flickr_dongkwan_540812245--Shrimp_Tostada.jpg"}},"cuisine/muisca-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/muisca-cuisine","n":"Muisca cuisine","c":"#94bd5c","crumb":[["The Americas","cuisines#the-americas"],["Colombian cuisine","cuisine/colombian-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in Colombia","intro":[["Muisca cuisine describes the food and preparation the Muisca elaborated. The Muisca were an advanced civilization inhabiting the central highlands of the Colombian Andes (Altiplano Cundiboyacense) before the Spanish conquest of the Muisca in the 1530s. Their diet and cuisine consisted of many endemic flora and fauna of Colombia."],["The main product of the Muisca was maize, in various forms. The advantage of maize was that it could be grown in the various climatic zones the Muisca territories experienced. It was the basis for their diet and the alcoholic drink, chicha, made from fermented maize and sugar."],["In the Muisca religion their agriculture and celebration of harvests, conducted along the complex Muisca calendar, were protected by Chaquén and Nencatacoa. The Muisca ate a variety of roots and tubers and even had a specific word in their Chibcha language for eating those: bgysqua."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"muisca-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Muisca_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Colombian cuisine","cuisine/colombian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Colombian cuisine","cuisine/colombian-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Cuisine of the Americas","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/1/15/Muisca_raft_Legend_of_El_Dorado_Offerings_of_gold.jpg/500px-Muisca_raft_Legend_of_El_Dorado_Offerings_of_gold.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Andrew Bertram","lic":"CC BY-SA 1.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/1.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Muisca_raft_Legend_of_El_Dorado_Offerings_of_gold.jpg"}},"cuisine/manchu-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/manchu-cuisine","n":"Manchu cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Northeastern Chinese cuisine","cuisine/northeastern-chinese-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in China","intro":[["Manchu cuisine or Manchurian cuisine is the cuisine of Manchuria (Northeast China) and Outer Manchuria (also known as Russian Manchuria). It uses the traditional Manchu staple foods of millet, soybean, peas, corn and broomcorn. It relies heavily on preserved foods (often pickled) due to the harsh winters and scorching summers in Northeast China. Manchu cuisine is also known for grilling, wild meat, strong flavours and the wide use of soy sauce. Manchu cuisine is more wheat-based than Han Chinese cuisines."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"manchu-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manchu_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Northeastern Chinese cuisine","cuisine/northeastern-chinese-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Northeastern Chinese cuisine","cuisine/northeastern-chinese-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine"},"cuisine/hangbang-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/hangbang-cuisine","n":"Hangbang cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Zhejiang cuisine","cuisine/zhejiang-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in China","intro":[],"src":null,"wiki":"Hangbang cuisine","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"hangbang-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hangbang_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Zhejiang cuisine","cuisine/zhejiang-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Land of Fish and Rice: Recipes from the Culinary Heart of China","ref":"cookbook/land-of-fish-and-rice-recipes-from-the-culinary-heart-of-china","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Fuchsia Dunlop, 2016","note":"Jiangnan cookbook that includes classic Hangzhou dishes.","url":"https://wwnorton.com/books/Land-of-Fish-and-Rice/","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Zhejiang cuisine","cuisine/zhejiang-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine"},"cuisine/liechtensteiner-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/liechtensteiner-cuisine","n":"Liechtensteiner cuisine","c":"#cf5f87","crumb":[["Europe","cuisines#europe"],["German cuisine","cuisine/german-cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of Liechtenstein","intro":[["The cuisine of Liechtenstein is diverse, having been influenced by the cuisine of nearby countries, particularly Switzerland and Austria, as well as other countries in Central Europe. Cheeses and soups are integral to Liechtensteiner cuisine, and milk products are also commonplace in the country, due to an expansive dairy industry. Common vegetables include greens, potatoes, and cabbage, while widely consumed meats include beef, chicken and pork. The consumption of three meals a day is commonplace, and meals are often formal."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"liechtensteiner-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Liechtensteiner_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["German cuisine","cuisine/german-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["German cuisine","cuisine/german-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"European cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/2/2c/K%C3%A4skn%C3%B6pfle.jpg/500px-K%C3%A4skn%C3%B6pfle.jpg","w":500,"h":216,"by":"Abigela 1234","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:K%C3%A4skn%C3%B6pfle.jpg"}},"dish/androlla":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/androlla","n":"Androlla","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Galician cuisine","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["An androlla is a Galician sausage. It is a type of embutido made from pork. It is similar to the botillo sausages of León."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-androlla"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Androlla"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Galician cuisine","cuisine/galician-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":3,"links":[["Paprika","ingredient/paprika"],["Pork","ingredient/pork"],["White wine","ingredient/white-wine"]]},{"h":"More from Galician cuisine","links":[["Caldo galego","dish/caldo-galego"],["Embutido","dish/embutido"],["Lacón","dish/lacon"],["Polbo á feira","dish/polbo-a-feira"],["Tarta de Santiago","dish/tarta-de-santiago"]]}]},"dish/boukete":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/boukete","n":"Boûkète","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Belgian cuisine","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["A boûkète is a type of Belgian pancake made with buckwheat flour, pan-fried in lard and frequently embellished with raisins. Boûkètes may be eaten hot or cold, garnished with a local brown sugar known as cassonade or with sirop de Liège."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-boukete"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bo%C3%BBk%C3%A8te"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Belgian cuisine","cuisine/belgian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":3,"links":[["Buckwheat flour","ingredient/buckwheat-flour"],["Lard","ingredient/lard"],["Raisin","ingredient/raisin"]]},{"h":"More from Belgian cuisine","links":[["Algérienne sauce","dish/algerienne-sauce"],["Belgian chocolate","dish/belgian-chocolate"],["Bickyburger","dish/bickyburger"],["Flemish stew","dish/flemish-stew"],["Friterie","dish/friterie"],["Liège waffle","dish/liege-waffle"],["Moules-frites","dish/moules-frites"],["Paling in 't groen","dish/paling-in-t-groen"],["Samurai sauce","dish/samurai-sauce"],["Speculoos","dish/speculoos"],["Steak tartare","dish/steak-tartare"],["Waterzooi","dish/waterzooi"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/2/26/Bo%C3%BBketes-rac.jpeg/500px-Bo%C3%BBketes-rac.jpeg","w":500,"h":332,"by":"Lucyin","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bo%C3%BBketes-rac.jpeg"}},"dish/chicha-morada":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/chicha-morada","n":"Chicha morada","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Peruvian cuisine","dot":"#94bd5c","intro":[["Chicha morada (literally, Purple Chicha) is a beverage from the Andean regions of Perú that is currently consumed at an international level."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-chicha-morada"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chicha_morada"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Peruvian cuisine","cuisine/peruvian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":1,"links":[["Purple corn","ingredient/purple-corn"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Peru Delights","ref":"site/perudelights-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"perudelights.com","note":"Morena Cuadra's blog of Peruvian home recipes and food culture.","url":"https://perudelights.com/tag/peruvian-home-cooking/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"Eat Peru","ref":"site/eatperu-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"eatperu.com","note":"Guide to Peruvian dishes and ingredients with a recipe section.","url":"https://www.eatperu.com/category/recipes/?sf_paged=2","suggested":true,"kind":"site"}]},{"h":"More from Peruvian cuisine","links":[["Anticucho","dish/anticucho"],["Coca tea","dish/coca-tea"],["Locro","dish/locro"],["Peruvian ceviche","dish/peruvian-ceviche"],["Picarones","dish/picarones"],["Stuffed peppers","dish/stuffed-peppers"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/f/fa/Chicha_Morada_2017.jpg/500px-Chicha_Morada_2017.jpg","w":500,"h":592,"by":"young shanahan from Bogotá, Colombia","lic":"CC BY 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chicha_Morada_2017.jpg"}},"dish/chrain":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/chrain","n":"Chrain","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish found in 3 cuisines","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["Horseradish (Armoracia rusticana, syn. Cochlearia armoracia) is a perennial plant of the family Brassicaceae (which also includes mustard, wasabi, broccoli, cabbage, and radish). It is a root vegetable, cultivated and used worldwide as a spice and as a condiment. The species is likely native to Southeastern Europe and Western Asia."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-chrain"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chrain"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["German cuisine","cuisine/german-cuisine"],["Thuringian cuisine","cuisine/german-cuisine-thuringia"],["Russian cuisine","cuisine/russian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":1,"links":[["Horseradish","ingredient/horseradish"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Quick German Recipes","ref":"site/quick-german-recipes-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"quick-german-recipes.com","note":"Long-running site by German-born cook Gerhild Fulson (Oma) with traditional German home recipes.","url":"https://www.quick-german-recipes.com/about-me.html","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"Dirndl K