-rinderwurst"],["Zopf","dish/zopf"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/c/ca/Food_013_white.JPG/500px-Food_013_white.JPG","w":500,"h":344,"by":"Albertyanks Albert Jankowski (original file) ; Off-shell (this version)","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Food_013_white.JPG"}},"ingredient/malic-acid":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/malic-acid","n":"Malic acid","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 2 dishes across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Malic acid is an organic compound with the molecular formula HO2CCH(OH)CH2CO2H. It is a dicarboxylic acid that is made by all living organisms, contributes to the sour taste of fruits, and is used as a food additive. Malic acid has two stereoisomeric forms (L- and D-enantiomers), though only the L-isomer exists naturally. The salts and esters of malic acid are known as malates. The malate anion is a metabolic intermediate in the citric acid cycle."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-malic-acid"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Malic_acid"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["British cuisine","cuisine/british-cuisine",[["Digestive biscuit","dish/digestive-biscuit"],["Jammie Dodgers","dish/jammie-dodgers"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Sodium bicarbonate","ingredient/sodium-bicarbonate"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Tartaric acid","ingredient/tartaric-acid"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"],["Butter","ingredient/butter"],["Leavening agent","ingredient/leavening-agent"],["Malt extract","ingredient/malt-extract"],["Wholemeal","ingredient/wholemeal"],["Apples","ingredient/apples"],["Raspberries","ingredient/raspberries"],["Raspberry jam","ingredient/raspberry-jam"],["Shortbread","ingredient/shortbread"],["Strawberries","ingredient/strawberries"]]}]},"ingredient/pachyrhizus-erosus":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/pachyrhizus-erosus","n":"Pachyrhizus erosus","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 2 dishes across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Pachyrhizus erosus, commonly known as jícama or Mexican turnip, is a native Mesoamerican vine, although the name jícama most commonly refers to the plant's edible tuberous root. It is in the pea family (Fabaceae). Pachyrhizus tuberosus and Pachyrhizus ahipa are the other two cultivated species in the genus. The naming of this group of edible plants can sometimes be confusing, with much overlap of similar, or the same, common names."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-pachyrhizus-erosus"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pachyrhizus_erosus"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine",[["Es buah","dish/es-buah"],["Karedok","dish/karedok"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Cinnamon","ingredient/cinnamon"],["Cucumis melo","ingredient/cucumis-melo"],["Honeydew (melon)","ingredient/honeydew-melon"],["Pandan","ingredient/pandan"],["Papaya","ingredient/papaya"],["Pineapple","ingredient/pineapple"],["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Cucumber","ingredient/cucumber"],["Holy basil","ingredient/holy-basil"],["Kencur","ingredient/kencur"],["Krupuk","ingredient/krupuk"],["Mung bean sprout","ingredient/mung-bean-sprout"],["Peanut sauce","ingredient/peanut-sauce"],["Shrimp paste","ingredient/shrimp-paste"],["Solanaceae","ingredient/solanaceae"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/9/95/Pachyrhizus_erosus_Blanco2.249.png/500px-Pachyrhizus_erosus_Blanco2.249.png","w":500,"h":747,"by":"Francisco Manuel Blanco (O.S.A.)","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pachyrhizus_erosus_Blanco2.249.png"}},"dish/green-goddess-dressing":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/green-goddess-dressing","n":"Green goddess dressing","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish found in 2 cuisines","dot":"#94bd5c","intro":[["Green goddess is a salad dressing, typically containing mayonnaise, sour cream, chervil, chives, anchovy, tarragon, lemon juice, and pepper."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-green-goddess-dressing"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green_goddess_dressing"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Cuisine of California","cuisine/cuisine-of-california"],["Cali-Mex cuisine","cuisine/cuisine-of-california-latino-and-hispanic-influence"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":8,"links":[["Anchovy","ingredient/anchovy"],["Black pepper","ingredient/black-pepper"],["Chervil","ingredient/chervil"],["Chives","ingredient/chives"],["Lemon","ingredient/lemon"],["Mayonnaise","ingredient/mayonnaise"],["Sour cream","ingredient/sour-cream"],["Tarragon","ingredient/tarragon"]]},{"h":"More from Cuisine of California","links":[["Celery Victor","dish/celery-victor"],["Hangtown fry","dish/hangtown-fry"],["Oysters Kirkpatrick","dish/oysters-kirkpatrick"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/8/82/Green_goddess_dressing.jpg/500px-Green_goddess_dressing.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Foodista","lic":"CC BY 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Green_goddess_dressing.jpg"}},"dish/haitian-patty":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/haitian-patty","n":"Haitian patty","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Haitian cuisine","dot":"#94bd5c","intro":[["A Haitian patty is a puff pastry commonly filled with beef, fish or chicken seasoned with epis. They are widely available in American cities with significant Haitian diaspora communities, including Brooklyn, New York; Miami, Florida; and Boston, Massachusetts."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-haitian-patty"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Haitian_patty"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Haitian cuisine","cuisine/haitian-cuisine"]]}